#### Response matrices

The particle and detector level \(N_{jet}\) that counted from the jets in PYTHIA sample as described above are used to build our response matrices. Because the number of \(J/\psi\) is used as the number of events of different \(N_{jet}\), we build our response matrices by the following procedures: (1) when particle and detector level \(J/\psi\) both exist, fill the response matrix with corresponding particle and detector level \(N_{jet}\); (2) if the particle level \(J/\psi\) exists, while the detector level \(J/\psi\) lost due to its rapidity selection, then this event is categorized as a missing event. The response matrices which constructed by using the jets with R=0.4 and R=0.6 are visualized as two-dimension diagrams in Fig. 5.75. Note that the missing events are not shown in these plots.

